 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**It's not just you, but probably not because Perth power prices have jumped.** The underlying price has barely moved. What changed is that the WA government's bill credits seem to have stopped, so you're now paying the full price.\n\n**Price change over the last year (ABS monthly CPI, year to August 2026)**\n- Perth electricity: **up 2.4%**\n- Australia-wide electricity: **up 13.2%**\n- Perth overall inflation: 4.3% (nationally 4.0%)\n\nSo Perth's actual price rise has been small, much smaller than the national one.\n\n**Why your bill still feels huge**\nThe ABS electricity index tracks what households pay after government credits. For Perth it has swung a lot:\n\n| Period | Perth electricity index |\n|---|---|\n| Months without credits, 2023–24 | ~95 |\n| Aug–Sep 2024 and Jan–Feb 2025 | **25.7** (credits covered roughly three-quarters of bills) |\n| Oct 2025–Jan 2026 | 69–90 (smaller credits) |\n| Feb–Jun 2026 | 100 |\n| Jul–Aug 2026 | **102.4** (full price, no sign of credits) |\n\nAt its cheapest in 2024–25, a typical Perth bill was about **a quarter** of what it is now. If you got used to bills shrunk or wiped out by credits, a full-price bill can look like it quadrupled, even though the price per unit only rose about 2–3% a year. The same effect is why some reports show Perth electricity \"up 250–289%\" in early 2026. Those figures compare against months when credits were cutting bills.\n\n**What this data can't tell you**\nThe CPI measures price only, not how much power you use. If your bill rose more than about 2–3% compared with a past period without credits, the cause is probably higher usage, like air-conditioning, heating, more people at home or new appliances. You can check by comparing the kWh on your recent bill with the same season a year earlier.\n\nIn short, Perth households have lost their bill credits, and electricity prices there have actually risen more slowly than in the rest of the country.",
